\title{A Selection-Theoretic Account of Content-Based\\ Importance Prediction in Budgeted Key Eviction}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{24 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Budgeted eviction --- retaining $B$ out of $n$ stored keys so as to preserve as much future attention mass as possible --- is a selection problem, not a regression problem. Yet the standard justification for a \emph{content probe}, a learned predictor of a key's future importance from the key's own vector, is regression-flavoured: report the coefficient of determination $\Rsq$ and argue that a more accurate score should evict better. We show that this reasoning is structurally invalid, and develop the selection theory that replaces it.

Working with a finite key set, a true importance vector $a$, a score $s$, and the notion of a \emph{top set} (a budget-$B$ set no member of which is scored below a non-member), we prove an exchange inequality from which everything follows: transfer theorems bounding the retention deficit of an $\varepsilon$-accurate score by $2B\varepsilon$ in $L^{\infty}$ and by $2\sqrt{B\cdot\SSE}$ in $L^{2}$, a sharpened form indexed by the number of exchanged keys, and the $\Rsq$-currency form $2\sqrt{B(1-\Rsq)\SStot}$. The $L^{\infty}$ constant is attained, hence unimprovable.

The central negative result is that these bounds are irreducibly one-sided. For every $\rho\in(0,1)$ --- in particular the measured $\rho=0.3185$ --- there is a score with $\Rsq$ exactly $\rho$ reproducing the oracle selection at \emph{every} budget. A four-key instance exhibits a score with squared error $150$ retaining one unit of mass while a score with squared error $1802$ retains nineteen. Prediction accuracy does not order retention, even weakly. We localise the true mechanism: the entire deficit is carried by mass in a $2\varepsilon$-band around the eviction cut-off, and vanishes identically when that band is empty.

For the mixture $h+\lambda p$ we prove selection stability is equivalent to a linear system in $\lambda$, giving a non-degradation threshold $\lambda\le\gamma/D$ (margin over probe oscillation) and showing the stable weight set is an interval --- the structural reason a harmful mixture harms monotonically. Explicit instances show a mixture strictly dominating both parents ($9<11<19$) with stability interval exactly $[0,2/5]$, so the helpful weight $\lambda=1$ lies outside the certifiably safe region: safety and usefulness are in exact quantitative tension.

Finally, for a sorted profile the length-$k$ prefix is a top set, so the retention knee is a floor for \emph{every} eviction policy; and a probe with error $\varepsilon$ run at budget at least the knee still reaches $\tau-2B\varepsilon$.
\end{abstract}

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} budgeted selection; key eviction; top sets; exchange inequality; coefficient of determination; retention transfer bounds; mixture stability; attention retention knee.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The setting}

An autoregressive attention model reading a long context accumulates a store of keys. Memory is finite, so a policy must decide which $B$ keys to retain. The quality of the decision is measured after the fact: how much of the attention mass that future queries actually direct at the store lands on keys the policy kept.

Let $\iota$ be a finite key set and $a:\iota\to\mathbb{R}$ the \textbf{true importance}, the total future attention each key receives. A policy outputs $S$ with $|S|=B$; its \textbf{retained mass} is
\[
\A(S)=\sum_{i\in S}a_i .
\]
The vector $a$ is unavailable at eviction time, so a policy is driven by a \textbf{score} $s:\iota\to\mathbb{R}$. Three families of surrogate are standard:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Accumulated heavy-hitter statistics}: $h_i$ is a running (possibly decayed) total of the attention key $i$ has already received. This is \emph{relational}.
\item \textbf{Content probes}: $p_i=\langle w,k_i\rangle$ for a learned $w$ and the key's own vector $k_i$. This is \emph{intrinsic}.
\item \textbf{Mixtures}: $h_i+\lambda p_i$ for a mixing weight $\lambda\ge 0$.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The motivating measurement}

The three arms were run on Python source at budget $B=64$ with identical methodology and budgets:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Arm & Retained mass at $B=64$\\
\midrule
Accumulated heavy-hitter & $0.9340$\\
Content probe only & $0.8149$\\
Hybrid, $\lambda=1$ & $0.9371$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

The probe's coefficient of determination against true importance averaged $\Rsq=0.3185$ (range $0.1225$--$0.5921$), against $0.329$ on English prose in the matched earlier round.

Code was chosen deliberately as the \emph{most favourable} terrain for a content probe: identifiers recur and syntax is rigid. The probe nonetheless loses $11.91$ points, and its $\Rsq$ is indistinguishable from the prose value. The mixture is \emph{non-degrading} on code ($+0.3$ points), whereas on prose the same mixture harmed monotonically in $\lambda$.

The natural narrative --- ``the probe loses because $\Rsq=0.32$ is low'' --- is what this paper refutes.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{itemize}
\item A minimal selection framework (\S\ref{sec:frame}) generated by a single exchange inequality.
\item Transfer theorems in $L^{\infty}$, $L^{2}$ and $\Rsq$ currency, a sharpened exchange-indexed form, and attainment of the $L^{\infty}$ constant (\S\ref{sec:transfer}, \S\ref{sec:sharp}).
\item The central negative result: $\Rsq$ does not determine retention (\S\ref{sec:neg}).
\item A second negative result: $L^{2}$ accuracy does not order retention even weakly (\S\ref{sec:neg}).
\item Localisation of the deficit to a $2\varepsilon$-band around the cut-off (\S\ref{sec:band}).
\item A complete stability theory of the mixture (\S\ref{sec:hyb}).
\item A bridge showing the retention knee is a floor for all policies (\S\ref{sec:knee}).
\item Two quantitative readings of the measurements (\S\ref{sec:meas}).
\end{itemize}

\section{The selection framework}\label{sec:frame}

\begin{definition}[Retained mass]
For $a:\iota\to\mathbb{R}$ and finite $S\subseteq\iota$, $\A(S)=\sum_{i\in S}a_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Top set]
A finite $S\subseteq\iota$ is a \emph{top set for the score $s$ at budget $B$}, written $S\in\Top(s,B)$, if
\[
|S|=B \qquad\text{and}\qquad \forall\,i\in S,\ \forall\,j\notin S:\ s_j\le s_i .
\]
\end{definition}

This is exactly the output of a greedy budget-$B$ evictor driven by $s$. Ties are permitted, so $\Top(s,B)$ may contain several sets; this non-uniqueness is the locus of the whole phenomenon studied below.

\begin{lemma}[Uniqueness under strict separation]\label{lem:uniq}
If $|T|=B$ and $s_j<s_i$ for all $i\in T$, $j\notin T$, then $\Top(s,B)=\{T\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Let $S\in\Top(s,B)$ and suppose $i\in T\setminus S$. Equal cardinality makes the symmetric difference balanced, so $S\setminus T\ne\emptyset$; take $j$ in it. Then $s_j<s_i$ by strict separation, while $s_i\le s_j$ by the top-set property of $S$ --- a contradiction. Hence $T\subseteq S$, and equal cardinality gives $S=T$.
\end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:uniq} makes every numerical instance below unambiguous: statements of the form ``\emph{every} selection driven by $s$ retains exactly $m$'' are meaningful and non-vacuous.

\begin{theorem}[Exchange inequality]\label{thm:exch}
Let $S\in\Top(s,B)$ and $|T|=B$. Then
\[
\sum_{i\in T\setminus S}s_i \;\le\; \sum_{i\in S\setminus T}s_i .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $|S|=|T|$ we have $|S\setminus T|=|T\setminus S|=:m$. If $m=0$ both sides vanish. Otherwise let $i_0$ minimise $s$ on $S\setminus T$ and $j_0$ maximise $s$ on $T\setminus S$. As $i_0\in S$ and $j_0\notin S$, the top-set property gives $s_{j_0}\le s_{i_0}$, whence
\[
\sum_{i\in T\setminus S}s_i \le m\,s_{j_0}\le m\,s_{i_0}\le\sum_{i\in S\setminus T}s_i. \qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Top sets maximise score mass]\label{cor:maxscore}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:exch}, $\sum_{i\in T}s_i\le\sum_{i\in S}s_i$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Add $\sum_{i\in S\cap T}s_i$ to both sides and use $T=(T\cap S)\sqcup(T\setminus S)$, $S=(S\cap T)\sqcup(S\setminus T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Oracle bound]\label{cor:oracle}
If $T\in\Top(a,B)$ and $|S|=B$ then $\A(S)\le\A(T)$.
\end{corollary}

This is the provable content of the experimental clause ``probe $\le$ accumulated $\le$ oracle'': the oracle inequality is a theorem, the ordering of the two heuristic arms is a measurement.

\section{Transfer theorems: accuracy buys a one-sided guarantee}\label{sec:transfer}

Write $\SSE(a,s)=\sum_i(a_i-s_i)^2$.

\begin{theorem}[$L^{\infty}$ transfer]\label{thm:linf}
Let $S\in\Top(s,B)$, $|T|=B$, and $|a_i-s_i|\le\varepsilon$ for all $i$. Then
\[
\A(T)-2B\varepsilon\;\le\;\A(S).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Decompose $\A(U)=\sum_{i\in U}s_i+\sum_{i\in U}(a_i-s_i)$ for $U\in\{S,T\}$. The score terms obey $\sum_T s\le\sum_S s$ by Corollary~\ref{cor:maxscore}, and each error term is bounded by $|U|\varepsilon=B\varepsilon$ in absolute value. Hence $\A(T)\le\sum_S s+B\varepsilon\le\A(S)+2B\varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpened $L^{\infty}$ transfer]\label{thm:sharpl}
Under the same hypotheses, $\A(T)-2\,|S\setminus T|\,\varepsilon\le\A(S)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply the same decomposition to the symmetric-difference blocks instead of to $S$ and $T$ wholesale. The common part $S\cap T$ cancels; Theorem~\ref{thm:exch} controls the score terms, and the error terms are bounded by $|S\setminus T|\varepsilon$ and $|T\setminus S|\varepsilon$, which are equal.
\end{proof}

Since $|S\setminus T|\le B$ this strictly strengthens Theorem~\ref{thm:linf}: a score that is inaccurate but agrees with the rival arm on most keys is nearly harmless. The number of exchanged keys, not the budget, is the natural scale.

\begin{lemma}[Block Cauchy--Schwarz]\label{lem:cs}
For any $U$, $\bigl|\sum_{i\in U}(a_i-s_i)\bigr|\le\sqrt{|U|\cdot\SSE(a,s)}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[$L^{2}$ transfer]\label{thm:ltwo}
Let $S\in\Top(s,B)$ and $|T|=B$. Then $\A(T)-2\sqrt{B\cdot\SSE(a,s)}\le\A(S)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
As in Theorem~\ref{thm:linf}, with Lemma~\ref{lem:cs} in place of the $L^{\infty}$ bound.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
Let $\bar a=\frac{1}{|\iota|}\sum_i a_i$, $\SStot(a)=\sum_i(a_i-\bar a)^2$, and, when $\SStot\ne 0$, $\Rsq(a,s)=1-\SSE(a,s)/\SStot(a)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Retention gap of an $\Rsq$-accurate probe]\label{thm:rsqgap}
If $S\in\Top(s,B)$, $|T|=B$ and $\SStot(a)\ne 0$, then
\[
\A(T)-\A(S)\;\le\;2\sqrt{B\bigl(1-\Rsq(a,s)\bigr)\SStot(a)} .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitute $\SSE=(1-\Rsq)\SStot$ into Theorem~\ref{thm:ltwo}.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:rsqgap} is the only rigorous statement connecting a probe's reported $\Rsq$ to its retention. It is an \emph{upper} bound on the deficit; \S\ref{sec:neg} shows no lower bound of comparable form exists.

\section{$\Rsq$ cannot be the mechanism}\label{sec:neg}

\subsection{An $\Rsq$-mediocre probe with perfect retention}

\begin{definition}[Shrinkage probe]
For $c\in\mathbb{R}$ set $\mathrm{sh}_c(a)_i=\bar a+c\,(a_i-\bar a)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:shsse}
$\SSE(a,\mathrm{sh}_c(a))=(1-c)^2\SStot(a)$, hence $\Rsq(a,\mathrm{sh}_c(a))=1-(1-c)^2$ when $\SStot\ne0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Termwise, $a_i-\mathrm{sh}_c(a)_i=(1-c)(a_i-\bar a)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:shorder}
For $c>0$, every budget $B$ and every $S$: $S\in\Top(\mathrm{sh}_c(a),B)\iff S\in\Top(a,B)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$x\mapsto\bar a+c(x-\bar a)$ is strictly increasing, hence order-preserving; the top-set condition is a conjunction of order comparisons.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[$\Rsq$ does not determine retention]\label{thm:rsqfail}
Let $\SStot(a)\ne0$ and $\rho\in(0,1)$. There exists a score $s$ with
\[
\Rsq(a,s)=\rho \qquad\text{and}\qquad \Top(s,B)=\Top(a,B)\ \text{ for every }B .
\]
In particular $s$ attains oracle retention at every budget, with $\Rsq$ exactly the measured $0.3185$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $s=\mathrm{sh}_c(a)$ with $c=1-\sqrt{1-\rho}$. Lemma~\ref{lem:shsse} gives $\Rsq=1-(\sqrt{1-\rho})^2=\rho$. Since $\rho\in(0,1)$, $\sqrt{1-\rho}<1$, so $c>0$ and Lemma~\ref{lem:shorder} applies; oracle retention follows from Corollary~\ref{cor:oracle}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
$\Rsq$ measures the \emph{magnitude} of the residual; retention depends on its \emph{direction} --- on which retained/discarded pairs it reverses. The shrinkage probe's residual $(1-c)(a_i-\bar a)$ is perfectly aligned with $a-\bar a$ and reverses nothing. Consequently the $11.91$-point probe deficit is not deducible from $\Rsq=0.3185$: any causal account of it must be an account of misordering near the cut-off.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Accuracy does not even order retention}

\begin{example}\label{ex:inv}
Let $\iota=\{0,1,2,3\}$ with
\[
a=(10,9,1,0),\qquad h=(1,2,3,4),\qquad p=(40,30,20,10),
\]
so $\SSE(a,h)=81+49+4+16=150$ and $\SSE(a,p)=900+441+361+100=1802$.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[Accuracy does not order retention]\label{thm:accinv}
For Instance~\ref{ex:inv}, $\SSE(a,h)<\SSE(a,p)$, yet
\[
S\in\Top(h,2)\implies\A(S)=1,\qquad T\in\Top(p,2)\implies\A(T)=19 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$h$ separates $\{2,3\}$ strictly and $p$ separates $\{0,1\}$ strictly, so by Lemma~\ref{lem:uniq} the top sets are unique: $\{2,3\}$ and $\{0,1\}$, with masses $1+0=1$ and $10+9=19$.
\end{proof}

Thus no implication ``smaller $\SSE$ $\Rightarrow$ at least as much retained mass'' can hold, even weakly: accuracy order and retention order are incomparable. Theorems~\ref{thm:rsqfail} and \ref{thm:accinv} are exact companions --- the first says a fixed accuracy is compatible with the \emph{best} retention, the second that a strictly better accuracy is compatible with strictly \emph{worse} retention.

\section{Where the deficit lives: the boundary band}\label{sec:band}

\begin{theorem}[Boundary-band bound]\label{thm:band}
Let $a_i\ge0$ for all $i$, $S\in\Top(s,B)$, $|T|=B$, $|a_i-s_i|\le\varepsilon$ for all $i$, and let $\mu$ satisfy $a_j\le\mu$ for every $j\notin T$. Then
\[
\A(T)-\A(S)\;\le\;\sum_{\substack{i\in T\\ a_i\le\mu+2\varepsilon}}a_i .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
We show $T\setminus S\subseteq\{i\in T: a_i\le\mu+2\varepsilon\}$. Let $i\in T\setminus S$; balanced cardinality yields some $j\in S\setminus T$. Then $s_i\le s_j$ by the top-set property of $S$, and $a_j\le\mu$ since $j\notin T$, so
\[
a_i\le s_i+\varepsilon\le s_j+\varepsilon\le a_j+2\varepsilon\le\mu+2\varepsilon .
\]
Finally $\A(T)-\A(S)=\sum_{T\setminus S}a-\sum_{S\setminus T}a\le\sum_{T\setminus S}a$ by non-negativity, and the inclusion gives the claim.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[No band, no loss]\label{cor:noband}
If in addition $a_i>\mu+2\varepsilon$ for every $i\in T$, then $\A(T)\le\A(S)$: the $\varepsilon$-accurate score loses nothing.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Oracle recovery under a margin]\label{cor:margin}
If $|T|=B$, $a_j+\gamma\le a_i$ for all $i\in T$, $j\notin T$, and $2\varepsilon<\gamma$, then every $S\in\Top(s,B)$ equals $T$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
For $i\in T$, $j\notin T$: $s_j\le a_j+\varepsilon\le a_i-\gamma+\varepsilon<a_i-\varepsilon\le s_i$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:uniq}.
\end{proof}

The deficit is thus carried entirely by keys within $2\varepsilon$ of the eviction cut-off; the \emph{safe core} of $T$ is always retained. The controlling statistic is the \textbf{band mass} $\sum_{i\in T,\,a_i\le\mu+2\varepsilon}a_i$, a \emph{local} quantity. Unlike $\Rsq$, band mass is free to differ substantially between two domains whose fits are numerically identical --- the theory's concrete prediction about the code/prose asymmetry.

\section{The transfer constant is sharp}\label{sec:sharp}

\begin{example}\label{ex:sharp}
Let $\iota=\{0,1,2,3\}$ with $a=(1,1,-1,-1)$ and the flat score $s\equiv0$.
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness of $2B\varepsilon$]\label{thm:sharpness}
For Instance~\ref{ex:sharp}: $|a_i-s_i|\le1$ for all $i$; $\{2,3\}\in\Top(s,2)$; $|\{0,1\}|=2$; and
\[
\A(\{2,3\})=\A(\{0,1\})-2\cdot 2\cdot 1 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$|a_i|=1$ everywhere gives $\varepsilon=1$; every key is scored $0$, so every pair is a top set, in particular $\{2,3\}$. The masses are $-2$ and $2$, and $-2=2-4$.
\end{proof}

Hence no inequality $\A(T)-cB\varepsilon\le\A(S)$ holds with $c<2$: Theorem~\ref{thm:linf} is exactly tight. The pessimism of the transfer theorems is a fact about the problem, not an artefact of the proof, and the worst case is attained by a maximally tied score. Ties, again, are the mechanism.

\section{The mixture: stability, thresholds, and the safety/usefulness tension}\label{sec:hyb}

Define the \textbf{hybrid score} $\hyb(h,p,\lambda)_i=h_i+\lambda p_i$, so $\hyb(h,p,0)=h$.

\begin{theorem}[Stability is a linear system]\label{thm:hybiff}
For any $h,p$, budget $B$, set $S$ and weight $\lambda$,
\[
S\in\Top(\hyb(h,p,\lambda),B)\iff |S|=B \text{ and } \forall i\in S,\ j\notin S:\ \lambda(p_j-p_i)\le h_i-h_j .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The top-set condition reads $h_j+\lambda p_j\le h_i+\lambda p_i$, which rearranges as displayed.
\end{proof}

Thus $S$ survives the mixture exactly on the solution set of $|S|\cdot(|\iota|-|S|)$ linear inequalities in the single variable $\lambda$.

\begin{theorem}[Non-degradation threshold]\label{thm:margin}
Suppose $|S|=B$, the accumulated score separates $S$ by a margin $\gamma$ (i.e.\ $h_j+\gamma\le h_i$ for all $i\in S$, $j\notin S$), and the probe has oscillation at most $D$ (i.e.\ $p_i-p_j\le D$ for all $i,j$). Then for every $\lambda\ge0$ with $\lambda D\le\gamma$,
\[
S\in\Top(\hyb(h,p,\lambda),B).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For $i\in S$, $j\notin S$: $\lambda(p_j-p_i)\le\lambda D\le\gamma\le h_i-h_j$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:hybiff}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:margin} there is $S'\in\Top(\hyb(h,p,\lambda),B)$ with $\A(S')=\A(S)$ --- namely $S'=S$. The hybrid can always match the accumulated arm.
\end{corollary}

Non-degradation is therefore a property of the \emph{accumulated} score's margin relative to the \emph{probe's dynamic range}, not of the probe's accuracy. Two domains with identical probe $\Rsq$ may differ completely in whether their mixtures degrade.

\begin{theorem}[Convexity of the stable weight set]\label{thm:convex}
If $S\in\Top(\hyb(h,p,\lambda_1),B)$ and $S\in\Top(\hyb(h,p,\lambda_2),B)$ then for every $t\in[0,1]$,
\[
S\in\Top\bigl(\hyb(h,p,(1-t)\lambda_1+t\lambda_2),B\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Fix $i\in S$, $j\notin S$ and set $\delta=p_j-p_i$, $\eta=h_i-h_j$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:hybiff}, $\lambda_1\delta\le\eta$ and $\lambda_2\delta\le\eta$. Multiply by $1-t\ge0$ and $t\ge0$ and add.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Order-connectedness]\label{cor:ordconn}
If $S$ is stable at $\lambda_1$ and at $\lambda_2$ and $\lambda_1\le\lambda\le\lambda_2$, then $S$ is stable at $\lambda$.
\end{corollary}

The stable weight set is thus an interval: once $\lambda$ leaves the stability interval of the accumulated selection it never re-enters. A domain in which the mixture harms can only harm \emph{monotonically} in $\lambda$. The monotone degradation observed on prose is structurally forced, not an empirical accident.

\begin{example}\label{ex:hyb}
With $a=(10,9,1,0)$, let
\[
h=(6,2,4,0),\qquad p=(2,7,2,5),\qquad \hyb(h,p,1)=(8,9,6,5).
\]
\end{example}

\begin{theorem}[A mixture can strictly dominate both parents]\label{thm:dominate}
At budget $2$ for Instance~\ref{ex:hyb},
\[
S\in\Top(h,2)\Rightarrow\A(S)=11,\quad S\in\Top(p,2)\Rightarrow\A(S)=9,\quad S\in\Top(\hyb(h,p,1),2)\Rightarrow\A(S)=19 ,
\]
realised by $\{0,2\}$, $\{1,3\}$ and $\{0,1\}$ respectively, with $9<11<19$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Each score separates its top pair strictly ($4>2$; $5>2$; $8>6$), so Lemma~\ref{lem:uniq} makes each top set unique; the masses are $10+1$, $9+0$ and $10+9$.
\end{proof}

The mechanism is transparent: $h$ misranks the pair $(1,2)$ and $p$ misranks the pair $(0,3)$; their sum repairs both. Strict dominance over both parents is a structurally available effect for mixtures of scores with disjoint error patterns --- not a rounding artefact. The measured $+0.3$-point gain on code sits in this r\'egime.

\begin{theorem}[Exact stability interval]\label{thm:interval}
For Instance~\ref{ex:hyb} and every $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$,
\[
\{0,2\}\in\Top(\hyb(h,p,\lambda),2)\iff\lambda\le\tfrac{2}{5}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:hybiff} the condition is the conjunction over $i\in\{0,2\}$, $j\in\{1,3\}$ of $\lambda(p_j-p_i)\le h_i-h_j$, i.e.\ $\lambda\le4/5$, $\lambda\le2$, $\lambda\le2/5$ and $\lambda\le4/3$. The binding constraint is $\lambda\le2/5$ and it implies the rest.
\end{proof}

Combining Theorems~\ref{thm:dominate} and \ref{thm:interval}: the accumulated selection is provably preserved exactly for $\lambda\in[0,2/5]$, while the weight that \emph{helps} is $\lambda=1$, outside that interval. Hence the deployment corollary: \emph{choosing $\lambda$ below the margin ratio $\gamma/D$ guarantees safety, and guarantees the hybrid learns nothing from the probe.} Any $\lambda$ small enough to be certifiably harmless is too small to alter a single selection. Non-degradation as measured is therefore not the same phenomenon as non-degradation as certified.

\section{The knee is a floor for every policy}\label{sec:knee}

Let $p:\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{R}$ be an antitone profile with retention curve $\R(k)=\sum_{i<k}p_i$ and
\[
\knee(p,\tau)=\inf\{k:\tau\le\R(k)\},
\]
the smallest budget at which top-$k$ eviction reaches the drift-assert threshold $\tau$. On code this came out at $12$ of $16$ heads, fewer than for prose.

\begin{lemma}[Prefixes are top sets]\label{lem:prefix}
Let $p$ be antitone, $k\le n$, and equip the $n$-key context with $a_i=p_i$. Then the prefix $P_k=\{0,\dots,k-1\}$ satisfies $P_k\in\Top(a,k)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$|P_k|=k$; for $i\in P_k$ and $j\notin P_k$ we have $i<k\le j$, so $a_j=p_j\le p_i=a_i$ by antitonicity.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The retention curve is the envelope of all policies]\label{thm:envelope}
For antitone $p$, $k\le n$ and any $|S|=k$: $\A(S)\le\R(k)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Below the knee, every policy fails]\label{thm:kneefloor}
If $B\le n$ and $B<\knee(p,\tau)$ then every $|S|=B$ has $\A(S)<\tau$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Experimental contrapositive]
If a policy at budget $B$ satisfies $\tau\le\A(S)$, then $\knee(p,\tau)\le B$: passing the drift assert certifies a knee bound.
\end{corollary}

The reported ``$12$ of $16$'' is therefore not a statement about the top-$k$ heuristic but an information-theoretic floor no scoring rule beats.

\begin{theorem}[Price of content-blindness at the knee]\label{thm:price}
Let $p_i\ge0$, $\knee(p,\tau)\le B\le n$, $\tau$ reachable, $S\in\Top(s,B)$ and $|a_i-s_i|\le\varepsilon$. Then $\tau-2B\varepsilon\le\A(S)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Monotonicity of $\R$ under non-negativity and $B\ge\knee$ give $\tau\le\R(B)=\A(P_B)$; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:linf} with $T=P_B$.
\end{proof}

With Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpness}, which attains $2B\varepsilon$, this pins the cost of content-blindness exactly: linear in budget and error, and no better. Sortedness is not needed for Theorem~\ref{thm:price} --- only the threshold reachability the knee supplies.

\section{Reading the measurements through the theory}\label{sec:meas}

\begin{theorem}[Dispersion lower bound]\label{thm:disp}
Let $S\in\Top(s,64)$, $|T|=64$, $\SStot(a)\ne0$, $\Rsq(a,s)=0.3185$, and $0.1191\le\A(T)-\A(S)$. Then $\SStot(a)>8\times10^{-5}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:rsqgap} gives $0.1191\le2\sqrt{64\cdot0.6815\cdot\SStot}$, so $(0.05955)^2\le43.616\,\SStot$, i.e.\ $\SStot\ge3.546\times10^{-3}/43.616>8\times10^{-5}$.
\end{proof}

The measured numbers are therefore not independent: a probe deficit of $11.91$ points at $\Rsq=0.3185$ and $B=64$ is simultaneously a measurement of the probe and a lower bound on the dispersion of the key population.

\begin{theorem}[Guarantee ratio: domain-universality, quantitatively]\label{thm:ratio}
\[
\frac{\sqrt{1-0.3185}}{\sqrt{1-0.329}}=\frac{\sqrt{0.6815}}{\sqrt{0.671}}<1.008 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$1.008^2\cdot0.671=0.68178\ldots>0.6815$; take square roots and use $\sqrt{1.008^2\cdot0.671}=1.008\sqrt{0.671}$.
\end{proof}

Since the bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:rsqgap} is proportional to $\sqrt{1-\Rsq}$ at fixed $B$ and $\SStot$, the guarantees licensed by the code and prose rounds differ by under $0.8\%$: at the level of the guarantee the two rounds are the same experiment.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{(A) Greedy budget-$B$ selection.} Sort keys by score descending and take the first $B$; cost $O(n\log n)$, or $O(n\log B)$ with a size-$B$ min-heap. Its output is a top set, so all transfer theorems apply verbatim.

\paragraph{(B) Stability-interval computation.} Given $h$, $p$ and $S$, the safe weights are $\{\lambda:\lambda(p_j-p_i)\le h_i-h_j\ \forall i\in S,\ j\notin S\}$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:hybiff}. Each pair with $p_j>p_i$ imposes the upper bound $(h_i-h_j)/(p_j-p_i)$; each pair with $p_j<p_i$ a lower bound; pairs with $p_j=p_i$ are vacuous or infeasible. The exact interval follows in $O(|S|(n-|S|))$ time --- for $B=64$, $n=1024$, about $6\times10^4$ comparisons, negligible beside a forward pass. This is the deployable safety check.

\paragraph{(C) Band-mass diagnostic.} With $\mu=\max_{j\notin T}a_j$ and $\varepsilon=\max_i|a_i-s_i|$, compute $\sum_{i\in T,\,a_i\le\mu+2\varepsilon}a_i$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:band} this upper-bounds the deficit; by Corollary~\ref{cor:noband} a zero value certifies zero loss. Cost $O(n)$. This is the statistic the theory recommends in place of $\Rsq$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the theory does and does not license}

The transfer theorems are \emph{guarantees}, not \emph{explanations}. They say a sufficiently accurate score cannot lose much; they do not say an inaccurate score must lose much, and three results show no such converse can be added: a fixed $\Rsq$ is compatible with perfect retention at every budget (Theorem~\ref{thm:rsqfail}); a strictly better $\SSE$ is compatible with strictly worse retention (Theorem~\ref{thm:accinv}); and the guarantee's constant is attained (Theorem~\ref{thm:sharpness}).

Three natural informal claims are therefore false as stated:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{``Low $\Rsq$ explains the 12-point probe deficit.''} False; the right statistic is a tie-weighted misordering rate near the cut-off, not a variance ratio. Theorem~\ref{thm:band} supplies the correct, local surrogate.
\item \emph{``Hybrid non-degradation is a property of the probe.''} False; Theorem~\ref{thm:margin} makes it a property of the accumulated score's margin relative to the probe's oscillation.
\item \emph{``An $L^{2}$-better score is a better evictor.''} False; the two orders are incomparable (Theorem~\ref{thm:accinv}).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{The law}

\begin{quote}
\textbf{Importance is relational, not intrinsic.} Even where identifiers repeat and syntax is rigid --- the most favourable terrain for a content-based predictor --- a key's own vector carries almost no information about its future reception. The observable that matters is how the key has already been used, not what it contains.
\end{quote}

The domain difference sits entirely in the \emph{interaction} term: content is neutral on code and harmful on prose. Combined with the knee analysis ($12$ of $16$ on code), the code-domain picture closes: fewer keys are needed, content is useless for choosing them, and recency plus accumulation remains the deployable pair.

\subsection{Scope and limitations}

The empirical anchor is a single language and repository at a single budget and model scale; the theory is domain-agnostic, the measured constants are not. Importance is treated as fixed once the future queries are fixed; online settings in which eviction changes the future distribution are out of scope. Non-negativity of $a$ is used only in Theorem~\ref{thm:band} and Corollary~\ref{cor:noband} (automatic for attention masses). All statements are worst-case over the rival selection $T$.

\section{Future directions}

\paragraph{1. Band mass, not $\Rsq$, is the domain-discriminating statistic.} The qualitative half is settled: Theorem~\ref{thm:band} proves the loss is at most the mass of the $2\varepsilon$-band around the cut-off. What remains is the empirical claim. Band mass is a \emph{local} quantity, so unlike $\Rsq$ it may differ between code and prose even when the fits are numerically identical, and it is directly measurable from an attention trace --- the prediction that code and prose have equal $\Rsq$ but unequal band mass at $B=64$ is testable with the existing harness.

\paragraph{2. The margin ratio predicts the sign of the hybrid interaction.} Theorem~\ref{thm:margin} makes non-degradation a function of $\gamma/D$. The conjecture is that measuring $\gamma$ and $D$ per domain predicts the observed sign of the interaction --- neutral on code, harmful on prose --- with no reference to $\Rsq$.

\paragraph{3. A tie-weighted misordering rate.} Theorems~\ref{thm:rsqfail} and \ref{thm:sharpness} jointly show the residual's size is the wrong summary. The proposed replacement weights each misordered retained/discarded pair by the mass at stake and the closeness of the tie; a two-sided bound in such terms would complete the theory.

\paragraph{4. Extension of the empirical programme.} Mathematical and non-English natural-language domains; learned online predictors rather than a fixed linear probe; increments at budget $4096$; and a $7$B-parameter cell to test scale-dependence of the margin statistics.

\section{Conclusion}

The claim that a content probe underperforms \emph{because} its $\Rsq$ is low is not merely unproven; it is unprovable, and three independent constructions show why. Retention is a selection functional, sensitive only to the ordering a score induces near the eviction cut-off, while $\Rsq$ is a magnitude summary blind to ordering. The correct localisation replaces the global variance ratio by the mass in a $2\varepsilon$-band around the cut-off --- a local statistic, and therefore one that can distinguish domains producing numerically identical fits.

For mixtures the picture is equally clean and less comfortable. Selection stability is a linear system in the mixing weight, so the safe region is an interval, harm is necessarily monotone once that interval is left, and safety is certified precisely by the condition $\lambda\le\gamma/D$ that also guarantees the probe contributes nothing. A mixture \emph{can} strictly dominate both parents --- the explicit instance retains $19$ against $11$ and $9$ --- but only by operating outside its certified region.

Beneath all of it lies a floor no policy escapes: the retention knee bounds every scoring rule, not just top-$k$ eviction. What a key is turns out to be nearly irrelevant to what it will be needed for; what it has already been used for is the signal that survives.

